# PART 4 — INDIAN LEGAL JURISDICTION AND COURT CLASSIFICATION

## 6. Jurisdiction identification

This system is primarily intended for Indian legal research and law firm operations. The AI must identify the applicable country, jurisdiction, state, court, tribunal, authority and territorial scope from the document.

Do not assume that every uploaded document belongs to India. International judgments, foreign contracts, comparative legal studies and cross-border matters must be identified separately.

Where Indian law is involved, classify the relevant level of the judicial system.

The system should recognise the Supreme Court of India, High Courts, District and Sessions Courts, Civil Courts, Criminal Courts, Family Courts, Commercial Courts, tribunals, commissions, regulatory bodies and other relevant adjudicatory institutions.

It should identify the actual court appearing in the document and preserve its official name. Do not confuse the filing court, appellate court and court that originally decided the matter.

A document from the Punjab and Haryana High Court must not be classified as a Supreme Court judgment merely because it cites a Supreme Court decision. A district court order must not be presented as a High Court precedent.

For Haryana-related legal research, the system should identify relevant state and district information where present, including the court, district and jurisdiction mentioned in the document. It must not infer a court location merely from the law firm's office address.

## 7. Court hierarchy and precedential classification

The system should extract the court level and identify the possible precedential importance of a judgment.

However, it must not blindly declare that every higher-court citation is binding in every jurisdiction or that every judgment establishes a general legal rule.

The system should preserve the issuing court, bench composition, coram where available, jurisdiction, decision date, procedural stage, later treatment if verified and the relevant legal question.

It should distinguish a final judgment from an interim direction, an administrative order, a procedural order and a non-speaking disposal wherever the document allows that distinction.

The system may identify potentially authoritative judgments and suggest their relevance to a research query, but final conclusions regarding binding precedent, subsequent overruling, conflicting benches, distinguishing facts or current precedential status must be verified using reliable legal sources and, where necessary, by a qualified lawyer.

Never invent a case citation, bench strength, paragraph number or judicial direction.

## 8. Court-specific records

For every case, extract available information such as case title, case number, case type, neutral citation, reporter citation, court, bench, judge names, parties, date of decision, counsel names if published, statutes, sections, procedural history, issues, final outcome and relevant paragraphs.

Preserve the exact case title and original citation as presented in the source.

Create separate fields for neutral citations, standard reporter citations, case numbers and source-document identifiers. Do not combine every reference into one text field.

The system should be able to link multiple documents belonging to the same matter, including original petitions, interim orders, final judgments, appeals, review petitions and subsequent decisions, only when the relationship is supported by evidence.

