# PART 5 — LEGAL PRACTICE AREA CLASSIFICATION

The most important classification requirement is that the AI must understand the legal subject matter of each document. It should not categorise a judgment only by the name of the statute or the court.

The same statute may appear in several types of litigation. One case may involve criminal procedure, constitutional rights, evidence and administrative action simultaneously. The system must identify the principal legal issue and store secondary legal subjects separately.

The following practice areas describe the classification framework. Inspect the actual practice areas configured in the PuniaAssociates website and map these categories to the existing structure. Do not replace the current website taxonomy without approval.

## 9. Constitutional Law

The Constitutional Law category should contain cases and research dealing with constitutional interpretation, fundamental rights, constitutional remedies, separation of powers, legislative competence, federalism, constitutional amendments, constitutional institutions and the relationship between the State and individuals.

The AI must identify the constitutional question actually raised in the case, the relevant constitutional provision, the challenged state action or legal provision, the petitioner's argument, the State's position and the court's reasoning. It should recognise whether the case concerns equality, freedom of speech, life and personal liberty, religious freedom, constitutional remedies, judicial review or another constitutional subject.

A judgment mentioning Article 14 does not automatically become an equality-law judgment. The AI must understand why the provision was invoked and whether the court actually decided that issue.

The system should extract relevant constitutional articles, legal tests, standards of review, reasoning and operative directions. It must distinguish a constitutional challenge from a case that merely cites a constitutional provision.

## 10. Civil Litigation and General Civil Disputes

Civil Litigation should contain disputes involving civil rights, claims, declarations, injunctions, recovery, possession, damages, specific performance, civil procedure, limitation, jurisdiction and enforcement of civil remedies.

The AI should identify the nature of the relief sought and the central civil issue. It should distinguish a suit for recovery from a title dispute, a declaration from an injunction and a procedural challenge from a decision on substantive rights.

Extract the court, suit number, parties, pleadings, issues framed, relief sought, evidence considered, findings and final decree or order where available.

A procedural judgment about adjournments or filing requirements should not automatically be presented as a precedent on substantive civil rights. Preserve the procedural context and decision stage.

## 11. Criminal Law and Criminal Litigation

Criminal Law should include criminal offences, investigations, prosecutions, bail, arrest, remand, trial, conviction, acquittal, sentencing, appeals, revisions, criminal procedure and criminal remedies.

The system must identify the difference between an allegation, an FIR, a charge sheet, a framed charge, evidence, a finding of guilt, a conviction and an acquittal. It must never treat an accused person as convicted merely because an FIR or complaint exists.

Extract applicable statutory provisions, offence descriptions, procedural stage, court, relevant evidence, issues, findings and final directions.

For cases involving bail, identify the grounds considered, relevant procedural posture and the court's actual reasoning. Do not invent general bail rules from one case without examining its facts and legal context.

The classification should recognise criminal cases under the applicable current and historical Indian statutory framework. It must not automatically convert older references into newer section numbers.

## 12. Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita and Criminal Substantive Law

This category should specifically organise research on criminal offences and substantive criminal law under the applicable Indian legislation, including the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita where relevant.

The AI must recognise the offences, definitions, exceptions, punishments and relevant statutory provisions actually discussed in the source.

Where a judgment concerns an offence under a predecessor enactment such as the Indian Penal Code, preserve the original provision. Do not silently substitute a Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita section merely because the newer legislation covers a broadly similar subject.

If a comparison between old and new provisions is requested, create a separately verified comparison record with clear citations to the respective statutory texts and commencement information.

## 13. Criminal Procedure and Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita

This category should cover arrest, investigation, police powers, remand, bail, summons, warrants, trial procedure, cognizance, discharge, framing of charges, appeal, revision and other procedural issues.

The system should recognise the applicable procedural enactment and preserve the exact section used in the original source.

For historical cases referring to the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973, retain those original references. Do not automatically replace them with provisions of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita.

When producing a cross-reference between the former and current law, the AI must verify the statutory mapping, commencement and applicable transitional provisions. A superficially similar heading is not enough to establish equivalence.

## 14. Evidence Law and Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam

Evidence Law should cover admissibility, relevancy, burden of proof, presumptions, documentary evidence, electronic records, witness examination, confessions, expert testimony, admissions, proof of documents, dying declarations and related questions.

The AI should identify the actual evidentiary proposition discussed by the court, the evidence considered and the legal standard applied.

For judgments referring to the Indian Evidence Act, preserve the historical statutory name and exact provision. For matters involving the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, preserve its actual provisions and applicable legal context.

Do not assume that every old Evidence Act provision has a direct one-to-one equivalent under the newer law. Cross-reference only after verification against authoritative statutory material.

## 15. Property Law and Real Estate

Property Law should cover ownership, title, possession, transfer, registration, conveyancing, partition, inheritance, tenancy, leases, mortgages, easements, land acquisition, property development and disputes involving immovable property.

The AI should identify the kind of property, relief sought, title documents, chain of ownership, registration issues, possession status and applicable statutory framework.

It must distinguish a title dispute from a contractual sale dispute, a tenancy matter from an ownership dispute and a land acquisition challenge from an ordinary transfer transaction.

Extract survey or property identifiers only when they genuinely appear in the source. Confidential property documents must not be published publicly.

## 16. Contract Law and Commercial Agreements

This category should include formation, validity, interpretation, performance, breach, remedies, damages, termination, indemnity, guarantees, representations, warranties and enforceability of agreements.

The AI should identify the parties, nature of agreement, obligations, disputed clauses, breach allegations, relief sought and court's interpretation.

Distinguish contractual obligations from statutory duties. Do not assume that a clause is enforceable merely because it appears in a signed document. Do not generate a final legal conclusion on enforceability without considering governing law, facts and relevant judicial authority.

For private contracts, the system must store the document in the confidential matter vault unless expressly approved for publication.

## 17. Corporate and Company Law

Corporate Law should include company formation, directors, shareholder rights, corporate governance, shareholder disputes, oppression and mismanagement, company restructuring, statutory compliance, corporate insolvency and company-related litigation.

The AI should identify the relevant entity, corporate status, directors, shareholders, statutory provisions, regulatory bodies and nature of dispute.

It should distinguish corporate litigation from routine business reporting. A judgment concerning director disqualification may belong under Corporate Law, while a general company investment announcement belongs under Business.

Preserve the exact statutory provisions and distinguish historical Companies Act provisions from later amendments and applicable rules.

## 18. Arbitration, Conciliation and Alternative Dispute Resolution

This category should include arbitration agreements, jurisdiction, appointment of arbitrators, interim protection, tribunal procedure, arbitral awards, enforcement, setting aside, conciliation and mediation.

The AI should identify whether a dispute concerns the existence or validity of an arbitration agreement, appointment of an arbitrator, jurisdiction of the tribunal, interim relief, challenge to an award or enforcement.

The system must distinguish a court's procedural direction from a final ruling on arbitral jurisdiction or the merits of an award.

For each judgment, extract the relevant arbitration provisions, procedural stage, issue and final direction. Do not treat all references to arbitration as equivalent legal principles.

## 19. Family Law and Matrimonial Disputes

Family Law should cover marriage, divorce, judicial separation, maintenance, custody, guardianship, domestic relationships, family property, inheritance and related family proceedings under the applicable legal framework.

The AI should identify the parties' legal relationship, applicable personal or statutory framework where clearly established, the relief sought, jurisdiction and procedural stage.

It must distinguish a maintenance proceeding from divorce, custody from guardianship and a domestic violence application from a general criminal complaint.

Family documents often contain highly sensitive personal information. These records should remain private by default. Never publish identities, personal correspondence, financial particulars or details about minors through public content generation without explicit legal and editorial approval.

## 20. Succession, Wills and Inheritance

This category should cover wills, probate, letters of administration, testamentary succession, intestate succession, inheritance rights, partition of inherited property, family arrangements and estate administration.

The AI should identify the relevant relationship, nature of succession, applicable statute, will status, disputed document and court's determination.

Do not assume that the same succession rules apply to every person or community. Personal law, statutory provisions, territorial jurisdiction and factual circumstances matter.

Preserve the exact statutory context and mark uncertain legal interpretations for qualified review.

## 21. Labour and Employment Law

Labour and Employment Law should include employment contracts, termination, wages, working conditions, industrial disputes, workplace rights, social security, employer responsibilities and employment-related litigation.

The AI should identify whether the matter concerns an individual service contract, industrial employment, statutory employment protections, disciplinary proceedings, compensation or a regulatory issue.

Extract the employment relationship, applicable legal framework, relevant dates, disputed action, remedies and court or authority.

Do not assume that every employment dispute is governed by the same legislation. Preserve the distinction between current and historical labour statutes and verify applicable commencement and transitional arrangements.

## 22. Service Law and Government Employment

Service Law should cover recruitment, appointments, eligibility, promotions, seniority, transfers, disciplinary action, suspension, dismissal, pension, reservation, service benefits and disputes involving public employment.

The AI must distinguish government service litigation from private-sector employment disputes.

Extract recruitment rules, notification dates, service regulations, public authority, post, eligibility conditions, relevant administrative decision and judicial relief.

A judgment about a recruitment examination may relate primarily to Service Law, Education or Administrative Law. Classify the principal issue and retain other subjects as secondary topics.

Do not invent eligibility criteria or dates. Where a judgment interprets an old recruitment rule, preserve the historical version and identify any later amendment only after verification.

## 23. Administrative Law

Administrative Law should contain judicial review of administrative decisions, natural justice, delegated legislation, administrative discretion, public authority obligations, government orders, licensing, regulatory powers and procedural fairness.

The AI must identify the challenged administrative action, competent authority, legal source of power, procedural issue and remedy sought.

Distinguish a policy decision from an individual administrative order, and an administrative recommendation from a final enforceable direction.

Extract the court's examination of jurisdiction, reasons, fairness, statutory authority and relevant procedural safeguards wherever the judgment discusses them.

## 24. Banking and Financial Law

This area should cover banking disputes, loans, guarantees, recovery proceedings, secured transactions, financial contracts, debt enforcement, borrower rights, financial regulation and disputes involving banks or lenders.

The AI should identify the nature of financial relationship, lender, borrower, security, loan type, alleged default, applicable legislation and forum.

Distinguish a civil recovery claim from insolvency proceedings and secured-creditor enforcement. Identify relevant statutory references carefully and preserve the legal stage.

Financial account details, customer identities and loan documents must be treated as confidential unless specifically authorised for public legal research.

## 25. Insolvency and Bankruptcy Law

This category should include corporate insolvency resolution, liquidation, individual insolvency where applicable, creditor claims, insolvency professionals, resolution plans, moratoriums and disputes under the applicable insolvency framework.

The AI should identify the relevant debtor, creditor type, insolvency stage, authority or tribunal, statutory provision and decision.

Distinguish admission of an insolvency application from commencement of a resolution process and distinguish a resolution plan from a completed resolution.

Preserve the relevant dates, procedural stage and actual operative directions. Do not assume that a party's claimed financial default has been legally established merely because insolvency proceedings have been initiated.

## 26. Consumer Protection Law

Consumer Law should cover defective goods, deficient services, unfair trade practices, product liability, consumer contracts, compensation, refunds, warranties and disputes before consumer commissions.

The AI should identify the consumer relationship, service or product, alleged deficiency, remedy sought, commission level and procedural stage.

Distinguish a commercial dispute from a consumer claim and preserve relevant statutory definitions and jurisdictional conditions.

Where the case concerns insurance, housing, banking or healthcare, record the main subject along with Consumer Law as an additional classification where appropriate.

## 27. Intellectual Property Law

Intellectual Property Law should include trademarks, copyright, patents, designs, trade secrets, passing off, infringement, licensing and disputes concerning protected creative or commercial rights.

The AI should identify the specific IP right, registration details if present, owner, alleged infringing act, relevant work or mark, relief and legal test considered.

Do not assume that every dispute concerning a brand name involves a registered trademark. Distinguish registration, ownership, use, passing off, licensing and infringement.

Preserve registration numbers and relevant dates exactly. Confidential commercial material and unpublished inventions should remain private.

## 28. Taxation and Revenue Law

This category should include direct taxes, indirect taxes, GST, customs, assessments, tax recovery, penalties, exemptions, refunds, tax appeals and disputes involving revenue authorities.

The AI should identify the relevant tax regime, assessment period, statutory provision, authority, demand or disputed liability and stage of proceedings.

It must distinguish a proposed demand, show-cause notice, assessment order, appeal, tribunal decision and final court judgment.

Historical tax matters must retain the law applicable to the relevant assessment period. Do not assume that a current amendment applies retrospectively.

## 29. Cyber Law and Data Protection

This category should include cybercrime, unauthorised access, online fraud, digital evidence, electronic records, privacy, data protection, intermediary liability and digital platform regulation.

The AI should identify the type of digital conduct, applicable technology, legal framework, relevant authority and procedural stage.

Distinguish a cybercrime prosecution from a data-protection compliance matter and from a contractual dispute involving a digital platform.

Protect passwords, personal identifiers, digital credentials, confidential communications and other sensitive information. Never expose such material in public article generation, legal search results or AI prompts that are not properly protected.

## 30. Environmental Law

Environmental Law should contain matters involving pollution, environmental permissions, regulatory compliance, forest and wildlife protection, land use, environmental impact assessment, natural resources and environmental public interest litigation.

The AI should identify the environmental issue, geographic location, challenged approval or activity, responsible authority, applicable statute and relief sought.

Distinguish scientific assertions in the evidence from legal findings by the court. Preserve the exact status of approvals, notices, clearances and directions.

## 31. Motor Accident Claims and Insurance Law

This category should include motor accident claims, compensation, liability, insurance coverage, policy interpretation, negligence, disability assessment, claims tribunals and related appeals.

The AI should identify the accident date, vehicle type, claimant, insurer, tribunal or court, injuries or loss as recorded in the source, legal issue and outcome.

Treat personal identity, medical particulars and financial information as confidential unless an approved public judgment can lawfully be used in a case analysis.

Distinguish an award by a tribunal from an appellate modification or final settlement.

## 32. Consumer Property, Tenancy and Housing Disputes

Where the actual content concerns housing projects, delayed possession, builder-buyer agreements, tenant rights, rent, eviction, development authority decisions or housing regulation, the system should identify the most relevant primary legal area.

It must distinguish ownership litigation from consumer deficiency, tenancy from service disputes and land acquisition from urban planning.

Preserve the specific statute, contractual relationship, authority and remedy sought.

Do not put every real estate case into Property Law if its actual central dispute concerns consumer rights or statutory regulation. Use a primary area with appropriate secondary classifications.

## 33. Education, University and Regulatory Law

This category should handle education-related litigation, admission eligibility, examination disputes, recognition, affiliation, university autonomy, disciplinary matters, regulatory approvals and institutional obligations.

The AI should distinguish policy disputes, service matters, student admission litigation, institutional regulation and examination challenges.

Extract the relevant educational institution, authority, course, examination, notification, academic year and applicable rule.

Do not turn one judicial order into a universal admission rule without checking its facts and jurisdiction.

## 34. Writ Petitions and Public Interest Litigation

Writ-related documents should be recognised as a procedural and constitutional form of legal proceeding, not automatically as an independent substantive subject.

The AI should identify the relevant writ jurisdiction, legal provision, public authority, nature of relief, challenged action and primary legal issue.

For public interest litigation, distinguish the petitioner’s claims, public interest question, maintainability issues and the court's final directions.

A writ petition concerning property may be primarily Property Law with Constitutional or Administrative Law as related categories. A writ concerning service matters may be Service Law with Writ Proceedings as a procedural label.

## 35. Tribunal and Regulatory Litigation

This category should cover litigation before specialised tribunals and regulatory authorities, including matters involving company law, securities, tax, consumer protection, service disputes, environmental regulation and other specialist areas.

The AI must identify the forum, jurisdiction, enabling statute, type of proceeding and appeal or review route where stated.

Do not assume that every tribunal order is a court judgment. Preserve its actual document type and issuing authority.

