# PART SEVEN — CASE LAW INTELLIGENCE ENGINE

## 40. Detailed judgment analysis

Every judgment uploaded to the platform should be converted into a structured legal research record.

Extract the case title, citation, case number, issuing court, bench, decision date, judges, parties, legal provisions, facts, procedural history, issues, arguments, legal reasoning, authorities cited, final holding and operative directions wherever available.

The system must preserve the difference between facts of the case, submissions of the parties, questions framed by the court, findings, reasoning and final operative order.

Do not treat a party's argument as the court's holding. Do not treat a quoted precedent as the present court's own conclusion. Do not confuse an obiter observation with an operative direction.

## 41. Ratio decidendi and obiter analysis

The AI may propose the probable ratio decidendi and identify passages that appear to be obiter dicta, but it must show the exact paragraph references supporting its analysis.

It should explain which legal issue was necessary for the court's decision and which observations may be broader than what was required to decide the dispute.

These outputs must be marked as AI-assisted legal analysis requiring verification. The platform must not present a model-generated legal interpretation as an authoritative judicial statement.

Where the judgment is short, unclear, split between opinions or procedurally limited, the AI must acknowledge those limitations.

## 42. Statutory section mapping

Identify every material Act and section discussed in the judgment.

Store the statute title, year, section, subsection, clause and whether the provision was central to the decision or merely mentioned.

Link the judgment to the correct Bare Act record and section-wise library.

Do not link to a similarly numbered provision from another enactment. A section number without its statute title is not a reliable identifier.

## 43. Citation extraction and verification

Extract every identifiable case citation from judgments, articles and research papers.

Maintain the original citation string and, where possible, normalised case identity. Identify whether a citation refers to the same case using an alternative citation format.

Where reliable legal sources are available, verify the citation, case title, court and date. Never fabricate a neutral citation or reporter reference.

If an identified citation cannot be verified, mark it as unverified rather than presenting it as confirmed.

## 44. Subsequent treatment and precedent history

Where reliable sources permit, associate judgments with subsequent appeals, review petitions, overruling decisions, clarifications, larger-bench decisions or later judgments that distinguish or follow them.

Do not claim that a judgment is overruled, reversed or no longer good law based merely on a similar later case.

Any subsequent-treatment classification must have a source and date. If the system cannot reliably establish the status, display it as not verified or research required.

