# PART EIGHT — LEGAL RESEARCH AND KNOWLEDGE RETRIEVAL

## 45. AI legal research search engine

Build a legal search interface for authorised users.

The search engine should support ordinary search, exact phrases, case citations, case numbers, statute names, section numbers, court names, parties, judges, dates, legal issues and natural-language questions.

A researcher should be able to ask questions such as:

"Find judgments dealing with rejection of a bail application under the relevant criminal procedure law."

"Show decisions discussing the validity of an arbitration agreement."

"Find cases interpreting the applicable principles of limitation in property disputes."

"Find judgments from the Punjab and Haryana High Court concerning service-law seniority disputes."

"Locate cases discussing admissibility of electronic evidence under the applicable evidence law."

These are search examples, not claims that relevant results necessarily exist.

The system should retrieve the relevant judgments, statutory provisions and source passages. It must provide case citations, court details, paragraph references and links to the original stored source where authorised.

## 46. Retrieval-grounded answers

The AI must answer legal research questions from the documents and verified sources actually available to the application.

Do not generate a legal conclusion merely from model memory when the application is expected to perform document-based research.

Every material legal proposition should be linked to supporting authorities or statutory text where available.

When sources conflict or no relevant source is present, state that the available material is insufficient.

The system must distinguish a search result, a summarised judgment, an AI interpretation and an official statutory provision. These are different kinds of legal information and should be visually distinguishable.

## 47. Multiple-document legal analysis

Allow authorised lawyers to select multiple judgments or statutes and request a comparison.

The AI should compare relevant facts, issues, statutory provisions, reasoning and outcomes, identifying similarities and differences with source references.

It should not create false consistency between judgments that concern different procedural stages or legal frameworks.

A comparison should preserve jurisdiction, court hierarchy, decision date and statutory version so that the legal researcher can evaluate the relevance of each authority.

