# PART NINE — LEGAL ARTICLES AND PUBLIC KNOWLEDGE PUBLISHING

## 48. Dedicated Legal Articles Library

Create a separate public-content system for approved legal articles.

Article types may include legal explainers, case-law analyses, statutory updates, legal news, practice-area guides, frequently asked legal questions, comparative law articles, legal research summaries, professional insights and educational resources.

The AI should identify the article's topic, format, jurisdiction, relevant statute, legal subject, author, date, relevant source and proposed practice area.

The article must be written or edited in a form appropriate for public legal information. It should not expose confidential client facts or imply that a general explanatory article is personalised legal advice.

## 49. Case-law analysis articles

When converting a judgment into an article, clearly identify the case, court, date, legal issue, reasoning and outcome.

Preserve the distinction between the judgment itself and the AI-generated or lawyer-written commentary.

The article should link to the original judgment wherever legally permissible and available. It must never invent the case's final holding, paragraph number or statutory interpretation.

## 50. Legal explainers and guides

When the uploaded material contains an explanation of a legal process, the AI should identify the relevant jurisdiction, applicable statute, eligibility or procedural conditions, available remedies and limitations.

For example, an explainer concerning bail must distinguish different types of bail and the procedural context. A guide concerning property registration must distinguish relevant registration requirements from ownership rights. A family-law guide must clearly identify that personal circumstances and applicable laws may change the analysis.

All legal guides must include an appropriate date or verification status because legislation and procedure can change.

## 51. Public FAQs

Extract frequently asked questions from approved materials or create proposed FAQs based on a lawyer-approved source.

Each FAQ should be concise, jurisdiction-specific and grounded in verified legal material.

Do not generate definitive answers where the legal question depends on specific facts that are not available.

Questions involving urgent litigation, personal legal rights, statutory limitation or complex legal interpretation should be flagged for lawyer review.

## 52. Practice-area pages

The system should support content organisation by the existing PuniaAssociates practice areas.

Each practice-area page may contain an approved introduction, services actually offered by the firm, relevant legal articles, selected public judgments, FAQs and contact information.

Never invent the firm's experience, case success rate, client list, credentials, awards, professional affiliations or practice capabilities.

Use only verified information already present in the approved firm profile or expressly supplied by an authorised administrator.

