# PART TEN — CONFIDENTIAL CLIENT MATTER VAULT

## 53. Absolute separation between public and confidential data

This is a critical requirement.

Do not publish client documents just because they are uploaded. A case petition, signed agreement, identity document, bank statement, legal notice, medical record, private correspondence, evidence file or draft pleading must be treated as confidential by default unless an authorised lawyer explicitly designates it for a permitted public use.

Create a separate private matter-management area with role-based access and matter-specific permissions.

Public website visitors must never be able to access client documents, private case metadata, internal notes, confidential AI analysis or private matter search results.

## 54. Private matter classification

The AI should be able to identify the client matter, document type, case number, court, parties, filing date, limitation-related date if present, hearing date if present, document status and responsible lawyer where explicitly available.

It should identify the document as a petition, affidavit, vakalatnama, written statement, agreement, legal notice, evidence, order, judgment, correspondence or other file type.

Do not invent missing dates or legal deadlines. Extract explicit dates and mark any derived deadline as an unverified suggestion requiring lawyer confirmation.

## 55. Confidentiality and security

Do not use client files for public search, public SEO, website articles or training-style data reuse.

Implement strict role-based access, secure storage paths, audit logs, access control checks, retention controls and privacy-conscious AI processing.

Do not send confidential documents to external model providers unless the service configuration, legal permissions, organisational policy and data-processing arrangements have been explicitly approved.

Provide a private AI-assisted document-summary option only for authorised users and preserve access restrictions throughout processing.

Any client confidentiality or privacy concern must result in a review hold rather than public publication.

