# PART FOURTEEN — AI LEGAL CLASSIFICATION AND QUALITY CONTROL

## 66. Explainable legal decisions

Every AI classification must retain the reason behind it.

For example, if a document is assigned to Service Law, the system should indicate the relevant employment relationship, public service rule, disputed administrative action and central issue supporting that classification.

If the same document also contains constitutional grounds, the system may store Constitutional Law as a secondary legal subject.

The output must distinguish an AI recommendation from a verified lawyer decision.

## 67. Independent confidence scores

Maintain separate confidence values for document type detection, text extraction, court identification, citation extraction, statute recognition, primary category, secondary category, source authenticity and privacy classification.

Do not combine them into one overall score that conceals uncertainty.

A document may have high OCR confidence but low court identification confidence. A judgment may be reliably identified but its ratio decidendi may require legal review. Each part should retain its own status.

## 68. Legal accuracy review

Never invent case citations, legal propositions, section numbers, statutory wording, court orders, commencement dates, case outcomes or lawyer credentials.

When legal sources conflict, preserve the conflicting references and flag the issue.

Distinguish historical laws from current laws. Preserve the date on which a judgment interpreted a statutory provision.

AI-generated legal summaries, research explanations and ratio analysis must be labelled as assisted analysis until approved.

