# PART FIFTEEN — SOURCE VERIFICATION AND LEGAL COMPLIANCE

## 69. Approved source hierarchy

For statutory texts and official legal notifications, prefer authoritative government sources and official publications.

For judgments, prefer the relevant Supreme Court, High Court, tribunal or official judicial source where available. Use reliable legal research databases only within their applicable access and licence conditions.

Maintain the original source URL, download date and document identity.

A document from an unofficial compilation must not automatically be labelled an official court copy. Source authenticity should be explicitly recorded.

## 70. Publication and professional conduct safeguards

Before creating any public legal content, inspect the currently applicable professional conduct rules, restrictions on legal advertising, client confidentiality requirements and other relevant professional obligations.

Do not create fabricated success claims, guaranteed outcomes, misleading comparative claims, unsupported expertise claims or solicitation-oriented content.

Do not publish client identities, confidential facts, case strategies or confidential documents without proper authorisation.

Do not automatically publish every legal article, judgment or legal update merely because it has been uploaded.

Maintain a lawyer approval gate before public publication of legal commentary or content that may materially influence a reader's understanding of legal rights.

## 71. Legal information disclaimer

Where appropriate, distinguish general legal information from personalised legal advice.

Do not create a disclaimer that falsely implies that all legal content is verified legal advice from the firm. Use clear, lawyer-approved wording consistent with the firm's professional and regulatory obligations.

Do not generate an attorney-client relationship claim or guarantee of representation merely because a user visits the website or submits a contact form.

