# PART SIXTEEN — TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE AND DATABASE DESIGN

## 72. Existing infrastructure integration

Use the existing PuniaAssociates application and its current Firebase and cloud configuration wherever possible.

Inspect whether the project uses Firestore, Firebase Authentication, Cloud Storage, Cloud Run, a Node.js server and other services.

Reuse the existing resources where appropriate. Do not create a second database, website or authentication system.

Any new collections or storage structures must be compatible with the existing application and must be documented before deployment.

## 73. Separate data domains

Keep the following logical data domains separate:

Public articles and legal publications.

Case law and public judicial materials.

Legislation and statutory references.

Lawyer profiles and firm information.

Private client matters and confidential documents.

Import jobs and processing reports.

AI-generated research outputs and review history.

Use the existing naming conventions and actual document models. These are conceptual domains, not mandatory invented collection names.

Apply strict access rules according to whether the content is public, internal or confidential.

## 74. Background processing architecture

Large imports must be processed outside a single long-running browser request.

Use a durable queue and authenticated worker architecture compatible with the existing Cloud Run project. Cloud Tasks or Cloud Run Jobs may be used if the actual project configuration supports them.

The job should proceed through discovery, extraction, classification, validation, metadata enrichment, duplicate checks, confidentiality screening, database writes and final reporting.

Store checkpoints and per-document processing status. Start with a conservative batch size and controlled concurrency. Support pause, resume, cancellation and retry without duplicate database writes.

Never rely on an in-memory process remaining active indefinitely.

## 75. Legal knowledge indexing

Create an indexing strategy that can retrieve judgments by case citation, court, statutory provision, legal subject, date, party, issue and source.

If semantic search or vector embeddings are added, preserve access permissions at every retrieval stage. Confidential client documents must never leak into public search results through embeddings, cached queries or related-document recommendations.

Store source references and exact paragraph or page locations so researchers can return to the underlying document.

