# PART SEVENTEEN — SECURITY AND CONFIDENTIALITY

## 76. Role-based access

The application should support appropriate roles such as super administrator, managing lawyer, authorised lawyer, research staff, content editor and other roles that are actually required by the firm.

Do not assume every employee should have access to every client matter.

Verify authentication and permissions on the server, not only in frontend navigation.

A hidden menu item is not an adequate security mechanism.

## 77. Secure file handling

Validate file types, actual MIME signatures, size limits and archive structures. Prevent ZIP path traversal and malicious file extraction.

Sanitise imported HTML, block unsafe scripts and validate links. Never execute document macros or arbitrary uploaded commands.

Store sensitive documents in protected cloud storage paths and enforce appropriate access rules.

Do not include client names, confidential case material or sensitive document contents in unsecured system logs.

## 78. AI provider and data privacy controls

Before sending a private matter document to an AI provider, inspect the available model configuration, data-processing terms, retention controls and security requirements.

Use only approved processing settings for confidential material.

Never place private documents in public or general-purpose model prompts without appropriate authorisation. Do not use confidential client material to generate publicly accessible summaries or examples.

